  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.4 x^2 + 0.6 z^2) / 2, drawn at 0.4 times the loss. x is the steep direction, so a learning rate above 2/2.4 ≈ 0.83 makes the x swings grow until the run diverges.",
    "Each step moves the ball by -rate × (true slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian, centred on zero, with spread 0.8 × sqrt((64 - b) / (63 b)) for batch size b: largest at 1, exactly zero at 64.",
    "Both arrows are steps (rate × slope), drawn at 3 times their true length, one scale for both. Mini-batch step is the move the ball will make; Full-data step is the move with no noise. They are hidden once the run ends.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished) or when the loss passes 4.8 or the ball leaves the drawn square (diverged). The trail holds the start plus one point per step (at most 41); off-bowl points and the ball are drawn at the rim.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed seed and a running counter. Reset replays the same sequence. New run, keep last path continues the sequence, so the noise differs. Steps run at five per second at normal speed."
  ]
}